{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778614421960,"sessionID":"ses_1e25ab9d4ffeFFuBus26tWAFrq","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"functions.task:20","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Research GCSH glycine encephalopathy","prompt":"Search the internet and PubMed for information about GCSH-related glycine encephalopathy and OMIM:620423 (multiple mitochondrial dysfunctions syndrome 7). I need to understand:\n1. Is OMIM:620423 about glycine encephalopathy or multiple mitochondrial dysfunctions syndrome?\n2. What is the relationship between GCSH mutations and glycine encephalopathy?\n3. Are there existing gene-specific glycine encephalopathy terms in OMIM (e.g., OMIM:605899 for GLDC-related, OMIM:620398 for AMT-related)?\n4. What is the ClinGen preferred label for OMIM:620423?\n\nPlease do web searches and summarize findings. Focus on authoritative sources like OMIM, ClinGen, PubMed. Return all findings concisely.","subagent_type":"general"},"output":"task_id: ses_1e258c244ffeSDGC0lD9TjazC4 (for resuming to continue this task if needed)\n\n<task_result>\nHere are the findings across authoritative sources:\n\n**1. Is OMIM:620423 about glycine encephalopathy or multiple mitochondrial dysfunctions syndrome?**  \n- **OMIM:620423 is titled “MULTIPLE MITOCHONDRIAL DYSFUNCTIONS SYNDROME 7; MMDS7.”** It is **not** a glycine encephalopathy entry per se.  \n- Mechanistically, GCSH encodes the H-protein, which has a **moonlighting / dual role**:  \n  - Component of the **glycine cleavage system** (glycine metabolism)  \n  - Required for **protein lipoylation** of mitochondrial enzymes (e.g., pyruvate dehydrogenase, α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase)  \n- Because of this dual function, biallelic GCSH variants produce a **combined phenotype**: nonketotic hyperglycinemia (glycine encephalopathy features) **plus** lipoate deficiency/mitochondrial dysfunction. The 2023 paper (Arribas-Carreira et al., *Hum Mol Genet* PMID:36190515) describes this as “**combined nonketotic hyperglycinemia and lipoate deficiency**.”\n\n**2. What is the relationship between GCSH mutations and glycine encephalopathy?**  \n- GCSH variants cause **nonketotic hyperglycinemia** because the H-protein shuttles the methylamine group in the glycine cleavage system. Loss of function leads to glycine accumulation in plasma and CSF.  \n- However, unlike classic GLDC- or AMT-related NKH, GCSH-related disease also involves **mitochondrial bioenergetic defects** (lipoate-dependent enzyme failure), leading to lactic acidosis, Leigh-like MRI changes, and broader mitochondrial dysfunction. Clinical spectrum ranges from neonatal fatal glycine encephalopathy to attenuated developmental delay/epilepsy with movement disorders.\n\n**3. Are there existing gene-specific glycine encephalopathy terms in OMIM?**  \n- **OMIM:605899** = **GLYCINE ENCEPHALOPATHY 1; GCE1** (**GLDC**-related, P-protein defect; ~80% of classic NKH)  \n- **OMIM:620398** = **GLYCINE ENCEPHALOPATHY 2; GCE2** (**AMT**-related, T-protein defect; ~20% of classic NKH)  \n- **There is no “Glycine Encephalopathy 3” for GCSH** in OMIM. OMIM has instead classified the GCSH-related phenotype under **MMDS7** (620423).  \n- Related phenotype: **OMIM:614462** = “Hyperglycinemia, Lactic Acidosis, and Seizures” (HGCLAS; lipoate cofactor disorders).\n\n**4. What is the ClinGen preferred label for OMIM:620423?**  \n- ClinGen’s **Aminoacidopathy Gene Curation Expert Panel** classifies the GCSH–disease relationship as:  \n  - **Disease name:** **multiple mitochondrial dysfunctions syndrome 7** (mapped to **MONDO:0957382**)  \n  - **Classification:** **Definitive**  \n  - **Inheritance:** Autosomal recessive  \n- Separately, ClinGen lists GCSH under **dosage sensitivity** for **glycine encephalopathy** (MONDO:0011612), but the **gene-disease validity curation labels the disorder as MMDS7**.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line:** OMIM:620423 is **MMDS7**, not a numbered glycine encephalopathy. GCSH mutations straddle both pathways (glycine clearance and mitochondrial lipoylation), producing a blended NKH-plus-mitochondrial phenotype. Classic gene-specific GE entries exist for GLDC (GCE1) and AMT (GCE2), but not for GCSH.\n</task_result>","metadata":{"sessionId":"ses_1e258c244ffeSDGC0lD9TjazC4","model":{"modelID":"moonshotai/Kimi-K2.6","providerID":"togetherai"},"truncated":false},"title":"Research GCSH glycine encephalopathy","time":{"start":1778613960137,"end":1778614421954}},"id":"prt_e1da71e48001tn74wOn5pn0Pct","sessionID":"ses_1e25ab9d4ffeFFuBus26tWAFrq","messageID":"msg_e1da70b3900190Z74Dat920PsG"}}
